<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zanabazar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1635">1635</a>–<a href="/years/1723">1723</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Öndör Gegeen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zanabazar was a Mongolian Buddhist leader and artist, the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gombodorj">Tüsheet Khan Gombodorj</a> and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebtsundamba-khutughtu">Jebtsundamba Khutughtu</a>, head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a>. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, he founded monasteries and in 1686 devised the Soyombo script. In 1691 he led the Khalkha princes in submitting to the Qing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a>. His gilded bronze sculptures of Buddhist deities are regarded as masterpieces of Mongolian art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zanabazar.jpg" alt="Zanabazar" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zanabazar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zanabanzar_self-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
